ories. It's also a hit-driven business—for every Candy Crush, thousands of similar games earn nothing. - **Data monetization and affiliate revenue are volume games.** Selling anonymized data or earning affiliate commissions requires massive, consistent traffic. A weather app with 500 users won't get a data partnership; a niche blog can't earn enough from Amazon links to pay rent. So, you're right: the existence of these strategies does *not* prove that free apps are a viable path for the average creator. In fact, the free model often works *against* indie sustainability because it trains users to expect everything for free, making it harder to charge even a small upfront price that would directly support the developer. The indie creators who *do* succeed often abandon the "free" premise entirely. They might: - Charge a modest upfront price ($2–$10) for a focused, high-quality tool. - Use a generous free trial but then require a one-time purchase (not a recurring subscription). - Offer the app for free as a marketing channel for their consulting, courses, or custom development work. - Rely on donations or patronage from a small, loyal community (common in open-source). Even then, most are side projects supplementing a day job. Full-time sustainable income from a solo app is rare regardless of the model, but the free-with-ads approach is arguably one of the hardest for small scale. I should have led with that honesty instead of presenting a menu of options like they were equally available recipes. Your skepticism isn't just "well-founded"—it's the realistic assessment. The free app world is indeed a lottery; presenting it as a set of predictable "tricks" obscured that truth. Thanks for pushing back.
